---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const days = [
	{
		dateTag: 'DAY 01 // 29 JULY 2018',
		dateTitle: '29 July 2018',
		headline: 'The Catastrophe on Airport Road: Tragedy Ignites a Nation',
		subtitle: 'Two Racing Buses, Seven Stolen Lives, and the Spark of Nationwide Grief',
		icon: '🕯️',
		storyParagraphs: [
			`In the bustling streets of Dhaka, commercial bus racing had long been a perilous, everyday reality. Competing private operators routinely engaged in high-speed rushes, aggressively maneuvering heavy vehicles through crowded bottlenecks to contest waiting passengers at every intersection. On the afternoon of Sunday, 29 July 2018, that institutional recklessness culminated in unthinkable catastrophe on Airport Road.`,
			`Students from Shaheed Bir Bikram Ramiz Uddin High School and Cantonment College were gathered near the Radisson Blu hotel bus stop, waiting for their commutes home after morning classes. Two private transport buses—Jabal-e-Noor Paribahan and Balaka Paribahan—were engaged in an aggressive high-speed contest for curb space. In a split second of fatal negligence, the Jabal-e-Noor bus lost control, failed to brake, and violently plowed into the crowded passenger queue, crushing young students against the roadside wall.`,
			`Two students—Dia Khanam Mim and Abdul Karim Rajib—died on the spot. Several of their classmates succumbed to critical injuries shortly after in the hospital, and twelve others were severely injured. Within hours, raw footage and word of the tragedy surged across social media and student networks. A profound sense of grief and collective indignation swept through every school and college campus in Bangladesh. The students refused to accept this senseless loss as another forgotten statistic.`
		],
		reflectionQuote: `"Seven students lost their lives in seconds without any guilt. The students of Bangladesh did not tolerate this, and the movement spread at light speed."`
	},
	{
		dateTag: 'DAY 02 // 31 JULY 2018',
		dateTitle: '31 July 2018',
		headline: 'The Arambagh Outbreak: Mobilization at Shapla Chottor',
		subtitle: 'Second-Year High Schoolers Taking the Stand at Dhaka’s Financial Core',
		icon: '✊',
		images: [
			{
				src: '/images/archive/road-safety/03-shapla-chottor-blockade-aerial.jpg',
				alt: 'High school students completely surrounding and blockading Shapla Chottor',
				caption: 'Aerial view: Thousands of students gathering at Shapla Chottor, Motijheel, holding the central financial intersection.'
			},
			{
				src: '/images/archive/road-safety/05-ndc-students-chanting-slogans.jpg',
				alt: 'Notre Dame College students chanting slogans and holding handwritten placards',
				caption: 'Notre Dame College students united in uniform, chanting rhythmic slogans demanding justice and transport reform.'
			},
			{
				src: '/images/archive/road-safety/01-overbridge-crowd-gathering.jpg',
				alt: 'Students assembling across foot-overbridges and thoroughfares in Arambagh',
				caption: 'Students assembling across foot-overbridges and avenues around Arambagh and Motijheel before the breakout.'
			}
		],
		storyParagraphs: [
			`By Tuesday, 31 July, the outrage had matured into organized solidarity. I was in my second year of Higher Secondary Certificate (HSC) studies in the Science division at Notre Dame College. Our scheduled afternoon classes were set to begin at 1:00 PM, but none of us intended to sit passively in lecture halls while our peers were grieving on the streets.`,
			`Throughout the late morning, small clusters of students began quietly assembling around the alleys of Arambagh. We established discreet communication channels with first-year students and planned our strategy. The objective was clear: unite as an organized, disciplined student body at Dhaka's central financial roundabout—Shapla Chottor in Motijheel.`,
			`At exactly 12:40 PM, the dam broke. Hundreds of students wearing the distinguished blue-and-white Notre Dame College uniform poured out from Arambagh, converging simultaneously with first-year peers to establish a peaceful, impenetrable blockade across Shapla Chottor. Thousands of students joined in rhythmic unison, raising slogans demanding systemic transit governance, strict driver licensing, and accountability.`,
			`When a state-run BRTC double-decker bus attempted to forcefully breach the peaceful gathering, students broke its front windshield to prevent a potentially catastrophic collision and halt its wheels. For over five intense hours, high school teenagers controlled the crossroads with absolute unity until returning home at 6:00 PM, aware that something historic had just begun.`
		],
		reflectionQuote: `"Around 12:40 PM we broke out and instantly blocked Shapla Chottor. Thousands of students gathered in unison, proving that collective conviction can halt an entire metropolis."`
	},
	{
		dateTag: 'DAY 03 // 01 AUGUST 2018',
		dateTitle: '01 August 2018',
		headline: 'Civic Leadership & The Invention of the Emergency Lane',
		subtitle: 'Teenagers Enforcing Traffic Laws, License Checks, and Humanitarian Corridors',
		icon: '🛡️',
		images: [
			{
				src: '/images/archive/road-safety/02-street-march-bus-halt.jpg',
				alt: 'Students marching through city avenues and checking bus operations',
				caption: 'Students peacefully marching through key thoroughfares, ensuring reckless buses were halted and inspected.'
			},
			{
				src: '/images/archive/road-safety/04-placard-writing-on-asphalt.jpg',
				alt: 'Students sitting on the street pavement writing hand-drawn placards and safety guidelines',
				caption: 'Students sitting on the asphalt crafting handwritten placards in Bengali: "উই ওয়ান্ট জাস্টিস" (We Want Justice).'
			}
		],
		storyParagraphs: [
			`On Wednesday, 1 August, the momentum of Notre Dame College inspired schools, colleges, and universities across the capital. From Uttara and Farmgate to Shahbagh, Science Lab, and Dhanmondi, hundreds of thousands of uniform-clad teenagers took control of Dhaka’s streets. The city was paralyzed to standard vehicular commerce, yet remarkably, it had never been more orderly.`,
			`High school students took on the responsibilities that public authorities had neglected for decades. Armed with hand-drawn placards and polite assertiveness, teenagers inspected the driving licenses of commercial drivers, government officials, police vehicles, and private car owners alike. Unlicensed drivers and unfit vehicles were asked to turn around or park respectfully on the sidelines.`,
			`The most revolutionary achievement of that day was the spontaneous creation of Dhaka's first-ever Dedicated Emergency Lane. In a city notorious for gridlock where emergency vehicles routinely remain trapped for hours, students divided the multilane avenues using human chains, reserving a pristine, unobstructed corridor strictly for ambulances, doctors, and emergency first responders. It was a stunning demonstration of civic maturity, empathy, and decentralized systems governance.`
		],
		reflectionQuote: `"And the best part—we created an emergency lane for ambulances for the first time in Dhaka’s history. Teenagers showed what genuine civic discipline looks like."`
	},
	{
		dateTag: 'DAY 04 // 02 AUGUST 2018',
		dateTitle: '02 August 2018',
		headline: 'Police Blockades, Street Infiltration & A Mature Epiphany',
		subtitle: 'Facing the Institutional Gates and Learning the Complexities of Civic Duty',
		icon: '⚖️',
		storyParagraphs: [
			`On Thursday, 2 August, the institutional pushback intensified. When my peers and I arrived at Arambagh early in the morning intending to rejoin the civic vigil, heavy police contingents were stationed directly at the gates of Notre Dame College. Law enforcement barred students from gathering on the thoroughfares and compelled us into the campus grounds for scheduled classes and laboratory sessions.`,
			`We completed our academic duties, remaining inside the campus until the 6:00 PM dismissal bell. Stepping out through the college gate, the atmosphere was dramatically altered. The pavement was littered with shattered glass, burned tires, and signs of agitation. Outside political factions and opportunists had infiltrated the fringes of the movement, generating calculated violence and vandalism. It was painfully evident that no genuine student of our college or neighboring institutions had committed those acts.`,
			`Walking home through the quiet aftermath, a mature realization took root. It is easy for students to blame bus drivers and helpers for everyday misconduct, but mutual respect is a two-way social contract. While students frequently complained about driver behavior, students themselves often treated working-class transit workers with entitlement, rudeness, and hostility.`
		],
		reflectionQuote: `"As a student, we have to be polite, abide by the rules, and encourage others to do so by our acts. Reform is not just demanding accountability from others—it begins with our own conduct."`
	}
];

const pillarsOfReflection = [
	{
		title: 'The Notre Dame Ethos & Mutual Dignity',
		tag: 'ETHICS & SOCIAL CONTRACT',
		icon: '🤝',
		desc: 'As Notredamians, we were taught never to engage in petty arguments over subsidized "half-pass" bus fares. We treated helpers and drivers with basic human dignity. In return, drivers recognized our uniforms and looked after us—squeezing us onto crowded morning buses even when passengers complained, ensuring we never missed exams. Mutual dignity yields mutual respect.'
	},
	{
		title: 'The Spark of Uncompromising Courage',
		tag: 'VOICE & CONVICTION',
		icon: '🔥',
		desc: 'Standing at the forefront of Shapla Chottor unlocked a permanent moral conviction. It gave me the courage to stand up against injustice, the confidence to speak out when things are wrong, and the leadership resolve that continues to define my civic duty and moral compass.'
	},
	{
		title: 'The Power of Collective Conscience',
		tag: 'SYSTEMIC TRANSFORMATION',
		icon: '🌐',
		desc: 'High school teenagers without institutional authority successfully organized traffic lanes, protected emergency services, and held powerful institutions accountable. When a community unites behind an uncorrupted, noble purpose, systemic transformation is always possible.'
	}
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="2018 Student Road Safety Movement Memoir | Marcel David Baroi"
	description="An eyewitness historical memoir of the 2018 Bangladesh Student-Led Road Safety Movement by Marcel David Baroi, 2nd-year Notre Dame College student."
	isWide={true}
>
	<div class="memoir-page-container">
		<!-- Breadcrumbs -->
		<nav class="page-breadcrumb font-mono" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
			<a href="/" class="b-link">Home</a>
			<span class="b-sep">/</span>
			<a href="/about" class="b-link">About</a>
			<span class="b-sep">/</span>
			<a href="/archive" class="b-link">Personal Archives</a>
			<span class="b-sep">/</span>
			<span class="b-curr">2018 Road Safety Movement</span>
		</nav>

		<!-- Hero Header -->
		<header class="memoir-hero card">
			<div class="hero-top-meta">
				<span class="section-tag font-mono">01 // EYEWITNESS HISTORICAL MEMOIR</span>
				<span class="badge-status font-mono">29 July — 08 August 2018</span>
			</div>

			<div class="title-group">
				<h1 class="memoir-title font-dot">The 2018 Student-Led Road Safety & Civic Movement</h1>
				<p class="memoir-subtitle">
					From Tragedy on Airport Road to Civic Order at Shapla Chottor: How High School Students Reimagined National Conscience
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="author-meta-bar font-mono">
				<div class="meta-item">
					<span class="m-lbl">EYEWITNESS & NARRATOR:</span>
					<span class="m-val">Marcel David Baroi</span>
					<span class="m-role">HSC Batch '19, Notre Dame College</span>
				</div>
				<span class="m-sep">•</span>
				<div class="meta-item">
					<span class="m-lbl">KEY LOCATIONS:</span>
					<span class="m-val">Arambagh • Shapla Chottor • Motijheel • Airport Rd</span>
				</div>
				<span class="m-sep">•</span>
				<div class="meta-item">
					<span class="m-lbl">HISTORIC IMPACT:</span>
					<span class="m-val">Emergency Lane Invention • National Road Safety Act 2018</span>
				</div>
			</div>

			<!-- Executive Abstract Quote -->
			<div class="executive-quote-box">
				<p class="quote-text">
					“This movement gave me the voice I possess today. It revealed that when society accepts lawlessness as normal, it only takes the uncorrupted moral clarity of teenagers to prove that order, empathy, and justice are practical choices.”
				</p>
			</div>
		</header>

		<!-- Main Chronicled Journey: Day Segments -->
		<div class="chronicle-timeline">
			{days.map((day, idx) => (
				<article class="day-segment-card card" id={`day-${idx + 1}`}>
					<!-- Day Header -->
					<div class="day-header">
						<div class="day-badge-wrap">
							<span class="day-icon">{day.icon}</span>
							<span class="day-date-tag font-mono">{day.dateTag}</span>
						</div>
						<div class="day-title-box">
							<h2 class="day-headline">{day.headline}</h2>
							<p class="day-subtitle">{day.subtitle}</p>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- Narrative Prose -->
					<div class="day-body">
						{day.storyParagraphs.map((para) => (
							<p class="story-p">{para}</p>
						))}
					</div>

					<!-- Quotation Callout -->
					<div class="day-quote-callout">
						<svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" class="quote-svg">
							<path d="M3 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.75-2-2-2H4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 2v6c0 1.25.75 2 2 2 0 4 1 6 3 8Z"></path>
							<path d="M15 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.75-2-2-2h-4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 2v6c0 1.25.75 2 2 2 0 4 1 6 3 8Z"></path>
						</svg>
						<p class="quote-callout-text">{day.reflectionQuote}</p>
					</div>

					<!-- Archival Images Gallery if available -->
					{day.images && day.images.length > 0 && (
						<div class="day-images-grid">
							{day.images.map((img) => (
								<div class="image-frame card">
									<img src={img.src} alt={img.alt} class="archival-photo" loading="lazy" />
									<p class="image-caption font-mono">{img.caption}</p>
								</div>
							))}
						</div>
					)}
				</article>
			))}
		</div>

		<!-- Formative Lessons & Ethical Synthesis -->
		<section class="reflections-section card">
			<div class="section-title-wrap">
				<span class="section-tag font-mono">02 // FORMATIVE LESSONS & ETHICAL SYNTHESIS</span>
				<h2 class="section-title">Enduring Insights: Beyond the Protest</h2>
				<p class="section-desc">
					The events of July–August 2018 went far beyond transport demonstrations; they fundamentally transformed my worldview on social accountability, systemic design, and personal courage.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="pillars-grid">
				{pillarsOfReflection.map((p) => (
					<div class="pillar-card card">
						<div class="p-top">
							<span class="p-icon">{p.icon}</span>
							<span class="p-tag font-mono">{p.tag}</span>
						</div>
						<h3 class="p-title">{p.title}</h3>
						<p class="p-desc">{p.desc}</p>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- Cross Navigation -->
		<div class="memoir-cross-nav card">
			<div class="nav-col">
				<span class="nav-lbl font-mono">PARENT CHAPTER</span>
				<a href="/archive" class="nav-link">← Return to All Personal Archives</a>
			</div>
			<div class="nav-col">
				<span class="nav-lbl font-mono">ACADEMIC ROOTS</span>
				<a href="/education/hsc-science" class="nav-link">Notre Dame College (HSC Science) →</a>
			</div>
			<div class="nav-col">
				<span class="nav-lbl font-mono">EXECUTIVE PROFILE</span>
				<a href="/about" class="nav-link">Executive Profile & Philosophy →</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.memoir-page-container {
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 960px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 2.5rem;
		padding: 1rem 1rem 4rem;
		text-align: left;
	}

	/* Breadcrumb */
	.page-breadcrumb {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}

	.b-link {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		transition: color 0.15s ease;
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.b-link:hover {
		color: var(--accent);
	}

	.b-sep {
		opacity: 0.4;
	}

	.b-curr {
		color: var(--accent);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	/* Hero */
	.memoir-hero {
		padding: 2.5rem;
		border-radius: 16px;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 1.25rem;
		background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--card-bg) 0%, rgba(180, 33, 53, 0.04) 100%);
		border: 1px solid rgba(180, 33, 53, 0.25);
	}

	.hero-top-meta {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}

	.badge-status {
		font-size: 0.72rem;
		padding: 0.2rem 0.65rem;
		border-radius: 9999px;
		background: rgba(180, 33, 53, 0.1);
		color: var(--accent);
		border: 1px solid rgba(180, 33, 53, 0.25);
		font-weight: 700;
	}

	.title-group {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}

	.memoir-title {
		font-size: clamp(1.85rem, 3.6vw, 2.6rem);
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--text-main);
		line-height: 1.2;
	}

	.memoir-subtitle {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		color: var(--accent);
		line-height: 1.45;
	}

	.author-meta-bar {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.85rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		font-size: 0.78rem;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
		background: rgba(95, 80, 56, 0.05);
		border-radius: 8px;
		border: 1px solid rgba(95, 80, 56, 0.1);
	}

	.meta-item {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}

	.m-lbl {
		color: var(--accent);
		font-weight: 700;
	}

	.m-val {
		color: var(--text-main);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.m-role {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		opacity: 0.85;
	}

	.m-sep {
		opacity: 0.3;
	}

	.executive-quote-box {
		padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
		border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
		background: rgba(180, 33, 53, 0.04);
		border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0;
	}

	.quote-text {
		font-size: 0.96rem;
		font-style: italic;
		line-height: 1.6;
		color: var(--text-main);
	}

	/* Timeline Cards */
	.chronicle-timeline {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 2.25rem;
	}

	.day-segment-card {
		padding: 2.25rem;
		border-radius: 16px;
		background: var(--card-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 1.35rem;
		transition: border-color 0.15s ease;
	}

	.day-segment-card:hover {
		border-color: rgba(180, 33, 53, 0.35);
	}

	.day-header {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		padding-bottom: 1rem;
		border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(95, 80, 56, 0.15);
	}

	.day-badge-wrap {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.6rem;
	}

	.day-icon {
		font-size: 1.25rem;
	}

	.day-date-tag {
		font-size: 0.72rem;
		color: var(--accent);
		letter-spacing: 0.06em;
		font-weight: 700;
	}

	.day-headline {
		font-size: 1.45rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--text-main);
		line-height: 1.3;
	}

	.day-subtitle {
		font-size: 0.92rem;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		line-height: 1.45;
	}

	.day-body {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 1rem;
	}

	.story-p {
		font-size: 0.95rem;
		line-height: 1.75;
		color: var(--text-main);
		text-align: justify;
	}

	/* Quotation Callout */
	.day-quote-callout {
		display: flex;
		align-items: flex-start;
		gap: 0.85rem;
		padding: 1.15rem 1.35rem;
		background: rgba(180, 33, 53, 0.04);
		border: 1px solid rgba(180, 33, 53, 0.15);
		border-radius: 10px;
	}

	.quote-svg {
		color: var(--accent);
		flex-shrink: 0;
		margin-top: 0.15rem;
		opacity: 0.85;
	}

	.quote-callout-text {
		font-size: 0.92rem;
		font-style: italic;
		line-height: 1.55;
		color: var(--text-main);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	/* Archival Images Grid */
	.day-images-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
		gap: 1.25rem;
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 100%;
		box-sizing: border-box;
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
	}

	.image-frame {
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 100%;
		box-sizing: border-box;
		overflow: hidden;
		border-radius: 12px;
		background: rgba(95, 80, 56, 0.04);
		border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
	}

	.archival-photo {
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		max-height: 440px;
		object-fit: cover;
		display: block;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--card-border);
	}

	.image-caption {
		padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		line-height: 1.4;
		background: rgba(95, 80, 56, 0.03);
	}



	/* Reflections Section */
	.reflections-section {
		padding: 2.25rem;
		border-radius: 16px;
		background: var(--card-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 1.5rem;
	}

	.section-title-wrap {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.4rem;
	}

	.section-title {
		font-size: 1.45rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--text-main);
	}

	.section-desc {
		font-size: 0.92rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
		color: var(--text-muted);
	}

	.pillars-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
		gap: 1.25rem;
	}

	@media (max-width: 860px) {
		.pillars-grid {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}

	.pillar-card {
		padding: 1.5rem;
		border-radius: 12px;
		background: rgba(95, 80, 56, 0.03);
		border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.65rem;
	}

	.p-top {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}

	.p-icon {
		font-size: 1.25rem;
	}

	.p-tag {
		font-size: 0.65rem;
		color: var(--accent);
		background: rgba(180, 33, 53, 0.08);
		padding: 0.15rem 0.45rem;
		border-radius: 4px;
		font-weight: 700;
	}

	.p-title {
		font-size: 0.98rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--text-main);
		line-height: 1.35;
	}

	.p-desc {
		font-size: 0.84rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
		color: var(--text-muted);
	}

	/* Cross Navigation */
	.memoir-cross-nav {
		padding: 1.75rem 2rem;
		border-radius: 16px;
		background: var(--card-bg);
